  "body": "Closes #6435.\n\n## Why\n\nBoards could only be created empty through the API. `POST /api/boards/items` took no geometry, so the size always came from `widgetDefaultSizes` and the position was always the next free cell \u2014 a dashboard could not be described in code. Search engines, integrations, groups, api keys and global settings were reachable from the interface only.\n\n## Board composition\n\n`POST /api/boards/items` now accepts an **optional** placement. Without it the behaviour is byte for byte what it was before:\n\n```json\n{ \"boardId\": \"...\", \"kind\": \"clock\", \"sectionId\": \"...\", \"xOffset\": 4, \"yOffset\": 2, \"width\": 6, \"height\": 5 }\n```\n\n`width`/`height` set the size, `xOffset`/`yOffset` an exact position, `sectionId` the target section. The values apply to every layout and the width is clamped to narrow breakpoints; a `layouts` array gives per breakpoint control instead. Overlaps are rejected with `400`.\n\nAdded around it:\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Items | `GET /api/boards/{id}/items`, `PATCH`/`DELETE /api/boards/{boardId}/items/{itemId}` |\n| Sections | `GET`/`POST /api/boards/{id}/sections`, `PATCH`/`DELETE .../sections/{sectionId}` |\n| Layouts | `GET`/`PUT /api/boards/{id}/layouts` |\n| Board | `GET /api/boards/{id}` returns layouts, sections and items at once |\n| Access | `GET /api/boards/{id}/permissions`, `PUT .../permissions/users`, `PUT .../permissions/groups` |\n| Portability | `GET /api/boards/{id}/export` \u21c4 `POST /api/boards/import` with `onConflict: fail\uff5cskip\uff5creplace` |\n\nIds inside a board document are local references and are remapped on import, so the same document can be applied repeatedly and to other instances.\n\n## Remaining services\n\nSearch engines, integrations, groups, api keys and server settings are exposed over OpenAPI and registered as MCP tools. `GET /api/config/export` \u21c4 `POST /api/config/import` cover a whole instance.\n\nThe configuration document keeps ids as they are \u2014 widget options reference other entities by id inside an opaque object, so remapping would silently break boards. Entities are instead matched by natural key when the id is unknown, which is what makes a document from a separately installed instance applicable at all: every instance seeds its own `everyone` group with a random id.\n\nIt deliberately contains no users, no group memberships, no secret values, no custom widgets and no media \u2014 that is documented, and the backup endpoints remain the way to make a complete copy. Those now accept an `ApiKey` header so a recovery job does not need a browser session.\n\n## Headless bootstrap\n\n`homarr api-keys create -u admin` prints a key without the web interface. Together with the existing `recreate-admin` that removes the last manual step from a first time setup. `api-keys list` and `delete` are there too.\n\n## Safety\n\n- **No database migration.** The schema already stores position and size in `item_layout`.\n- **Imports are atomic.** Statements are collected and validated first, then applied in one transaction, so a document with a broken reference never leaves a board half written. `replace` never deletes the board row, so home board settings and per user permissions pointing at it survive.\n- **Bounded work.** Grid coordinates and sizes in a document are capped, and the placement scan only tests slot edges and never looks past the last occupied row, so its cost does not depend on the column count.\n- **Backwards compatible.** Every new field is optional; existing endpoints keep their behaviour.\n\n## Testing\n\n`pnpm turbo typecheck`, `pnpm lint` and `pnpm format` are clean, docs build passes. 377 tests in the touched packages, including:\n\n- placement: explicit size and position, collisions, clamping to narrow breakpoints, dynamic sections as sub grids, regression on the previous automatic placement\n- import/export round trips, `skip`/`replace` idempotency, rollback on an invalid document, cross instance adoption\n- authorization: anonymous and unprivileged sessions against every new endpoint, asserting `404` rather than `403` where board existence must not leak\n\nThe ten failing files in a full run (`packages/db/test/*-migration`, `packages/integrations/test/*`) are testcontainers based and fail identically on a clean checkout here.\n\n## Notes for review\n\n- Documentation lives in `apps/docs` in this repo, so it is updated in tree rather than in `homarr-labs/documentation`. Happy to move it if that is the wrong place.\n- `POST /api/boards/import` keeps the strict board name rule because it creates a board like the interface does; `config/import` accepts any name because it restores. Both are documented.\n- The grid cap is 256. It covers oldmarr boards with room to spare, but if wider boards exist in the wild the number should be revisited.\n\n---\n\n- [x] Builds without warnings or errors (`pnpm build`, autofix with `pnpm format:fix`)\n- [x] Pull request targets `dev` branch\n- [x] Commits follow the [conventional commits guideline](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/)\n- [x] No shorthand variable names are used (eg. `x`, `y`, `i` or any abbrevation)\n- [x] Documentation is up to date\n- [x] When using AI; No temp files are checked in, the code style follows the rest of the project\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n- **New Features**\n  - Added CLI commands to list, create, and delete API keys, displaying new keys once.\n  - Added board import/export, placement validation, permissions management, and configuration import/export with conflict handling.\n  - Expanded API access for configuration, groups, integrations, search engines, settings, and API keys.\n- **Bug Fixes**\n  - Improved search engine form handling and validation.\n  - Backup export and import now support API-key authentication for automation.\n- **Documentation**\n  - Added guidance for automated backups and CLI-based API key management.\n  - Documented board automation, configuration transfer, service endpoints, and validation behavior.\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->",
